## Mathematical attempts and objections

Write a1=1 and a_n=a_(n-1)²+c^(2^(n-1)-1). Then
f_c^[n](0)=a_n/c^(2^(n-1)). For n>=2 its denominator is a square.
The source's sufficient criterion is that both
b_n^±=i(a_(n-1)±sqrt(a_n)) are nonsquares in Z[i] for every n>=3,
provided f_c^[2] is irreducible. The criterion is sufficient; a square
a_n or even a square b_n is not by itself a source-stability counterexample.

1. **Primitive factorization.** Put A=a_(n-1), s²=a_n, m=2^(n-1)-1.
Then (s+A)(s-A)=c^m, gcd(A,c)=1 and gcd(s+A,s-A) divides 2A.
Also b_n^+ b_n^-=c^m. Away from 1+i the two factors split the prime
divisors of c into disjoint groups. If one b is square, the exponents in
its allocated group must be even, since m is odd. This handles various
odd-valuation cases already in Theorem3.2; it does not handle arbitrary
even valuations. Requiring every prime exponent of c odd would narrow
the source claim. Norms discard the unit and conjugate-prime information;
a square norm is necessary for a Gaussian square, not sufficient.
For example 5 itself has square norm25, but no Gaussian
square equals5, since its imaginary part forces one coordinate zero and
5 is neither an integer square nor the negative of one. This correction
is kept explicitly to avoid importing a false diagnostic.

2. **Uniform tail cutoff.** Source Theorem1.9 reduces each parameter to
a finite check at a depth depending on |c|; its denominator is
log(sqrt(1+1/|c|)). The lower separation q(c)>sqrt(1+1/|c|) tends to1.
The displayed bound grows on the scale log2|c|, rather than giving a
uniform depth. A uniform margin q(c)>=1+delta, delta>0, was not proved.
Nor was a compatible argument controlling every remaining depth for
every parameter. Applying the finite test separately to a finite box
does not reach the universal quantifier in Conjecture1.5.

3. **A fixed finite modular cover cannot finish the numerator route.**
Let M>=1 be any integer divisible by all proposed integer moduli; for a
finite set of Gaussian moduli choose M divisible by each (their norms'
product suffices). Set c=-3M². This is a nonzero negative integer other
than-1 and not a Gaussian square. It also cannot have the exceptional
form alpha²(2i-alpha²): if alpha=x+iy then alpha²=u+iv with v=2xy even,
and the imaginary part of alpha²(2i-alpha²) is 2u(1-v).
For a real value u=0; the real part is v(v-2)>=0 because v is even.
Thus f_c^[2] is irreducible, using Proposition2.3 (also independently
checked by its quadratic-factor calculation). For every n, induction
in the recurrence gives a_n(c)=1 mod M. Consequently no one of the
chosen moduli can prove a_n nonsquare for this c, at any depth.
The reductions sqrt(a_n)=1 give b_n^+=2i=(1+i)² and b_n^-=0,
so the same local witness defeats this fixed square-obstruction route.
This is a proof-method obstruction only: the source already proves
stability of negative nonsquare integer c in Theorem5.3. It is not a new
full-scope discovery, a counterexample to 1.5, or an obstruction to
parameter-dependent primes or combined local/global methods.

4. **Low-level curves.** At n=3 the equation s²=a3 is the elliptic
curve s²=c³+c²+2c+1. Source Proposition2.7 already lists all Gaussian
integral c:0,-1,±2i,-89. For c=-89, a3=(835i)², but the two b values
are -835-88i and835-88i; their norm89³ is nonsquare. At n=4 the curve
s²=c7+c6+2c5+5c4+6c3+6c2+4c+1 has genus3. The published rational-field
proof uses its rational Jacobian, and does not classify Gaussian integral
points or arbitrary n. No valid Gaussian-point computation, rank bridge,
or all-genus argument was obtained here. Reproving only n=3 or n=4
would be a helper/case, ineligible even if new.

## Checks, falsifier and precise remaining gap

checks.py uses only integer pairs, exact multiplication, powers and integer
square roots. The Gaussian-square test is exact: with z=a+bi and
S=isqrt(a²+b²), first require S²=a²+b²; then u²=(S+a)/2 and 2uv=b,
and finally square the candidate to verify. For the second iterate,
the source criterion is evaluated without a search cutoff:
c=alpha²(2i-alpha²) iff alpha²=i±sqrt(-1-c) for a Gaussian square
on the right; square c is handled separately.

The box -40<=Re(c),Im(c)<=40, excluding0,-1, contains6,559 parameters,
6,479 satisfying the second-iterate criterion. Exact numerators for
n=3,...,8 give39,354 comparisons. Only c=±2i at n=3 produce square
numerators in that box; both have reducible second iterate. No eligible
square-b obstruction is found. Separate exact controls use c=-89 and
the public c=-2i witness, and ten congruence-family parameters. All are
finite diagnostics. They do not extend the source's existing computations.

A falsifier for the actual target would be one explicit Gaussian integer
c with a complete irreducibility certificate for f_c^[2] and a nontrivial
factorization over Q(i) of some f_c^[n], n>=3. No such witness was found.
For a proof, one still needs the universal all-c/all-n sufficient-square
exclusion (or a different complete irreducibility argument). The public
Conjecture1.7 refutation, finite tests and fixed-modulus obstruction provide
none of these. No PROOF.md is appropriate.

